%I A401275 #16 Oct 29 2026 20:46:17
%S A401275 100000010101,10000000001011,1000000000100011,1001000000000101,
%T A401275 10000000000001101,100000000000100111,100000000000110101,
%U A401275 100000000001001011,100000000100001101,1000000000000000111001,1000000000000010100001,1000000001000000100011,1000100000000000001001
%N A401275 Primes p such that q, the reversal of the decimal digits of p, is prime, q > p, and both p*q and p^3+q^3 are decimal palindromes.
%C A401275 A161721 requires a palindromic product; this sequence additionally requires a palindromic cube-sum. The prime pair 1021 and 1201 passes the product condition but fails the cube-sum condition:
%C A401275 1021*1201 = 1226221 (palindrome)
%C A401275 1021^3+1201^3 = 2796655862 (not a palindrome)
%H A401275 Brent Stevens, <a href="/A401275/b401275.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..191</a>
%H A401275 Martianus Frederic Ezerman, Bertrand Meyer and Patrick Solé, <a href="https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/journals/JIS/VOL18/Ezerman/eze3.html">On Polynomial Pairs of Integers</a>, Journal of Integer Sequences 18 (2015), Article 15.3.5.
%H A401275 Brent Stevens, <a href="/A401275/a401275.txt">Completeness proof and search method</a>
%H A401275 Brent Stevens, <a href="/A401275/a401275_1.txt">Python program reproducing 191 term table</a>
%H A401275 Brent Stevens, <a href="/A401275/a401275_2.txt">Required primality certificates </a>
%e A401275 For p = a(1) = 100000010101, its decimal reversal is q = 101010000001. Both numbers are prime.
%e A401275 p*q = 10101001020402010010101 and p^3+q^3 = 2030607363361639669361633637060302.
%e A401275 Both results are decimal palindromes.
%Y A401275 Cf. A020449, A085315, A155513, A161721.
